<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-ga-uniform-b-field" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Vector Potential for a Uniform Magnetic Field</title>
    <idx><term>vector potential</term></idx>

    <p>
        Consider a uniform magnetic field <m>\vec{B}=B_0\mathbf{e_z}</m>.
        We can use geometric algebra to write this as a bivector
            <md>
                \overset{\curvearrowleft}{B} = B_0(\mathbf{e_x}\wedge\mathbf{e_y}) \, .
            </md>
        We seek to define the conditions for a vector potential

            <md>
                \vec{A} : \overset{\curvearrowleft}{B} = \vec{\nabla} \wedge \vec{A} \, .
            </md>
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="sub-ga-gauge-examples">
    <title>Gauge Examples</title>
    <p>
        We can use the following gauge examples to define the vector potential.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-ga-uniform-b-field-1">
        <title>Symmetric Gauge</title>

        <p>
            The symmetric gauge preserves rotational symmetry of the magnetic field about the <m>z</m>-axis.
            The vector potential for this gauge is given by
                <md>
                    \vec{A}_\text{sym} = \frac{B_0}{2}(x\mathbf{e_y}-y\mathbf{e_x})
                </md>
        </p>
        <proof>
            <p>
                <md>
                    \vec{\nabla} \wedge \vec{A}_{\text{sym}} = \left(\frac{\partial A_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial A_x}{\partial y}\right) \mathbf{e_x}\mathbf{e_y} = \left(\frac{B_0}{2} - \left(-\frac{B_0}{2}\right)\right) \mathbf{e_x}\mathbf{e_y} = B_0 \mathbf{e_x}\mathbf{e_y}
                </md>
            </p>
        </proof>

    </example>

    </subsection>

</section>
